data.content:
Here's the lightest approach — use .NET 8's built-in **`TimeProvider`** via constructor injection (no DI container needed):

**Updated `InvoiceCalculator.cs`:**
```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Production usage** — pass `TimeProvider.System`:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider.System);
```

**In tests** — install `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` and use `FakeTimeProvider`:
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

No interface to write, no DI container required — `TimeProvider` is an abstract class built into the runtime. Just pass it through constructors. Repeat for your other classes that call `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
